::CW_U06_L03_Q01::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Readers cannot tell what the main character wants. Which revision should come first?{
=A. Add an action or line of dialogue that reveals the character's goal.#Correct. Motivation becomes clearer when readers see what the character wants.
~B. Add random weather.#Incorrect. Weather does not clarify the character's goal.
~C. Change the font.#Incorrect. Font does not solve character motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Delete the title.#Incorrect. The issue is character motivation, not title.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A scene meant to be suspenseful reaches the reveal in one sentence. What is the best revision?{
~A. Remove suspense entirely.#Incorrect. This works against the scene's purpose.
~B. Add an unrelated character.#Incorrect. This does not control pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Add action beats and sensory details before the reveal.#Correct. These slow pacing and build tension. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Change all names.#Incorrect. Names do not slow the reveal. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best clarifies conflict?{
~A. Add a list of furniture.#Incorrect. Furniture does not create conflict by itself.
~B. Remove the character's choice.#Incorrect. This weakens conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Use a random flashback.#Incorrect. Flashback should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Show what each character wants and why those wants cannot both happen.#Correct. Conflict becomes clear when competing wants are visible.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q04::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A draft accidentally shifts from first person to third person. What should the writer revise?{
~A. The weather description.#Incorrect. The problem is narration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Point of view consistency.#Correct. The writer should control the narrator's position.
~C. The title only.#Incorrect. The title does not fix point of view.
~D. The page number.#Incorrect. Page number is irrelevant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q05::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which explanation best supports portfolio value?{
~A. I changed something somewhere.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The story is nice.#Incorrect. This is unsupported praise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I revised the final action so readers understand why the character leaves.#Correct. This explains revision and reader effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. I typed it again.#Incorrect. Retyping is not craft evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q06::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which story problem requires structure revision?{
=A. The middle event happens before the setup that explains it.#Correct. Sequence problems require structural revision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. One typo in a clear final draft.#Incorrect. That is convention polish. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A purposeful short sentence.#Incorrect. This may be a style choice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A clear conflict.#Incorrect. Clear conflict is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q07::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A peer says the character's decision feels sudden. Which revision best responds?{
~A. Change the character's eye color.#Incorrect. This does not prepare the decision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Add a random sound.#Incorrect. A sound helps only if connected to the decision.
~C. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue may help reveal decision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Add a moment showing the character weighing the choice.#Correct. This prepares the decision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q08::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which is the best first step before revising a fiction scene?{
~A. Correct every comma before reading the scene.#Incorrect. Diagnose the scene first. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Identify the scene's job and main craft problem.#Correct. Diagnosis guides targeted revision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Change the ending randomly.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Delete all setting details.#Incorrect. Setting may be useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q09::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which feedback is most useful for narrative revision?{
~A. I like it.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Make it better.#Incorrect. This gives no target. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I understand the setting, but I do not know why Mara apologizes in the final scene.#Correct. This identifies a specific motivation issue. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The story exists.#Incorrect. This is not feedback. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q10::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best improves character development?{
~A. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation does not develop character by itself.
~B. Remove the character's goal.#Incorrect. That weakens development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Change the file name.#Incorrect. File name is irrelevant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Add a choice that reveals what the character values.#Correct. Character is shown through choices. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q11::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What should a writer do if feedback asks for more clarity but the mystery is intentional?{
=A. Clarify necessary motivation while preserving purposeful mystery.#Correct. This balances reader access and intended effect.
~B. Delete every mysterious detail.#Incorrect. Mystery may be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ignore all feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Make the scene more confusing.#Incorrect. Confusion weakens reader understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q12::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best controls point of view?{
~A. Add every character's private thoughts in one paragraph.#Incorrect. That creates accidental shifts. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove all pronouns.#Incorrect. This is not a practical POV revision.
=C. Keep only the narrator's thoughts in a first-person scene.#Correct. This maintains point-of-view consistency. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Add unrelated description.#Incorrect. Description does not fix POV. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q13::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which change is most likely final polishing rather than revision?{
~A. Adding motivation before the ending.#Incorrect. This is revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Correcting a misspelled word after the scene's structure is clear.#Correct. This is a convention polish. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Reordering events for clarity.#Incorrect. This is structural revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Adding conflict to a flat scene.#Incorrect. This is revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q14::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A scene has no clear conflict. Which revision is strongest?{
~A. Add a random object.#Incorrect. Objects matter only if tied to conflict.
~B. Remove every choice.#Incorrect. This weakens conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Change margins.#Incorrect. Margins do not create conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Add a difficult choice with consequences.#Correct. Conflict often comes from pressure and consequence.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q15::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which portfolio explanation is weakest?{
=A. I made it better.#Correct. This is too vague to show portfolio value.
~B. I revised the pacing so suspense builds before the door opens.#Incorrect. This is specific. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I clarified the character's motivation through an action beat.#Incorrect. This is specific. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I fixed the point of view so readers stay with Mara's experience.#Incorrect. This explains revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q16::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best improves pacing in a rushed emotional scene?{
~A. Remove the emotional moment.#Incorrect. This avoids the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Add unrelated backstory.#Incorrect. Backstory may slow but can distract. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Add a brief action beat before the character responds.#Correct. This slows the moment and lets emotion register.
~D. Change every verb randomly.#Incorrect. Revision should be targeted. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q17::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which answer best identifies a motivation problem?{
~A. Readers know the character wants forgiveness.#Incorrect. That is clear motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Readers cannot tell why the character refuses to speak.#Correct. The reason for action is unclear. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. The punctuation is correct.#Incorrect. This is not motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The setting is vivid.#Incorrect. This is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q18::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best preserves voice while using feedback?{
~A. Rewrite the whole story in a formal research style.#Incorrect. That may erase fiction voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Ignore the confusing action.#Incorrect. The feedback identifies a real issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove the narrator.#Incorrect. This is extreme. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Clarify the confusing action but keep the narrator's informal word choice where it supports voice.#Correct. This improves clarity without erasing voice. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q19::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A story's events are out of order by accident. What is the best revision target?{
=A. Sequence and structure.#Correct. Event order is a structural issue. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Font size.#Incorrect. Font does not reorder events. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Favorite color.#Incorrect. This is unrelated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Title decoration.#Incorrect. Decoration does not fix sequence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q20::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which sentence best explains a narrative revision?{
~A. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This lacks craft reason. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It is better now.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I added the note under the door so readers understand why Ellis leaves without speaking.#Correct. This names revision and reader effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. I made edits.#Incorrect. This gives no detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q21::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which craft area is most connected to "who tells the story"?{
~A. Font color.#Incorrect. Font color does not determine narrator. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Page count.#Incorrect. Page count is length. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Spelling only.#Incorrect. Spelling is conventions. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Point of view.#Correct. Point of view controls who tells or perceives the story.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q22::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision best develops fiction character?{
~A. Add random weather.#Incorrect. Weather only helps if tied to character.
=B. Add a meaningful choice under pressure.#Correct. Choices reveal character. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Remove all conflict.#Incorrect. Conflict often develops character. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Change the title punctuation only.#Incorrect. This does not develop character. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q23::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which issue should be revised before portfolio submission?{
=A. The central conflict is still unclear.#Correct. Portfolio-ready fiction needs clear conflict. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. A purposeful action beat slows suspense.#Incorrect. That may be effective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Dialogue reveals character clearly.#Incorrect. This is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Motivation is easy to follow.#Incorrect. This is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q24::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which answer best matches the problem to revision?{
~A. Problem: unclear POV; Revision: add random weather.#Incorrect. Weather does not fix POV. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Problem: unclear POV; Revision: control whose thoughts are included.#Correct. This correctly matches issue and fix. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Problem: rushed scene; Revision: delete character goal.#Incorrect. This weakens scene. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Problem: weak conflict; Revision: correct one typo only.#Incorrect. Typo correction does not build conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q25::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which response best shows Lesson 3 mastery?{
~A. I can change random details.#Incorrect. Revision must be targeted. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I can ignore character motivation.#Incorrect. Motivation matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I can diagnose a narrative craft problem, make a targeted revision, and explain the reader effect.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. I can polish spelling while leaving the story confusing.#Incorrect. Major craft problems must be fixed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
